WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.670 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  let me tell you about the time  that i forgot my dad's birthday it was a  few years ago and i'd woken up with  large bags under my eyes  the true mark of a grey's anatomy binge  i reach out to my phone to shut my alarm  and i'm greeted with a stream of  notifications  texts emails friends family social media  news  even my meditation app was grasping for  my attention  and so i did what i'd done a thousand  times before  i sat in bed and mindlessly scrolled  and this happened throughout the day i  felt present in my interactions but at  every buzz  ping and moment of free time i would  reach out for my phone  and so when my dad called me later that  day i looked at the time and it finally  hit me  that i was behind on work and so i  ignored his phone call  and decided that i would be productive i  put my phone away and opened my computer  and that's when it began again later  when i found out  that i missed his birthday i felt so  guilty i had reached a new low  that was the day that i came to terms  with my technology addiction  and so i did what most people say is the  solution to this addiction  i deleted social media and distanced  myself as far as possible from  technology  and for a while i felt great in fact i  was successfully unplugged for two years  but the longer that i was off the grid  the more i miss out on  i'm missing photos of my friends i'm  just staying up to date with the latest  pop culture  and i miss learning about the world  through others  my tech addiction and my digital detox  taught me  that we can expect individuals to take  isolated actions  that will lead to collective societal  change  we need to shift the responsibility from  responsible tech usage  to responsible tech creation  don't get me wrong technology is a  powerful force for good  over the last few years i've worked on  products that have touched  hundreds and millions of users and i've  seen firsthand the transformative power  of technology technology has  revolutionized our world  and made our daily lives easier it has  connected us  brought us joy and entertainment and  insparked our creativities in new ways  it will lengthen our lives and improve  our quality of care  but i also understand that there's a  cost to all of that  and truthfully i worry that in trying to  solve  some problems i will create even more  because it's not just me a lot of us  have difficulty stepping away from the  products we love  we spend five hours a day consuming  digital content  and 12 hours a day in front of a screen  that's half  of our lives and it's not just tech  addiction  over the last few years we've seen the  impact of technology all around us  from misinformation and polarization ai  bias  and privacy infringements the potential  to fuel our anxiety and depression the  potential to radicalize individuals and  destroy our democracy  all rooted in this perpetual dependence  left unchecked technology and its  downstream effects  are poised to be the greatest public  health crisis of our century  so how do we get the good without the  bad  well i've always believed that looking  at where we've been  can help us get to where we need to be  so let's take a look at a public health  crisis of last century  cars in the early 1900s  cars replaced horse-drawn carriages  bringing a wave of possibility  but over time more and more cars were on  the road and these cars were able to go  faster and faster  drivers didn't have licenses age limits  or sobriety requirements  cars didn't have seat belts airbags or  mirrors  and roads didn't have speed limits  traffic lights or crosswalks  the result people were dying at its peak  there were 35 deaths for every 10 000  cars  for context today this number is one  once a beacon of promise and possibility  cars were surrounded by the unintended  consequences  it created that's exactly where we are  with technology today  full of potential but surrounded by the  unintended consequences it's created  for cars the responsibility was shirked  for decades  auto manufacturers looked the other way  government was slow to act  and the onus was on consumers the  drivers and pedestrians to  be more careful look where they're going  ultimately it was a work of mavericks  and stand-out individuals and  organizations that led to the change we  see today  so what can we learn from this well  first  it went on for far too long it took 50  years for a simple safeguard like seat  belts to be commonplace  in cars with the reach and scale of  technology  we can't afford 50 years but i also  learned  that safety sells safety and  responsibility  is good business would you buy a car  today that didn't have seat belts or  airbags  as consumers are becoming more aware of  the downstream effects of technology  safe tech is a competitive advantage and  building  and retaining their trust will soon be  non-negotiable  but most importantly i learned that the  responsibility  is on us as builders to create  responsible tech  because saying that the solution to tech  addiction is for consumers to just  manage their screen time  is the same thing as saying the solution  to car casualties is for drivers to  forego seat belts and just be a little  bit more careful  technology needs to be responsible from  the onset so over the last year  i've talked to hundreds of people in  this space gen z teenagers  tech ethicist heads of product to  understand what we can do to build  responsible technology i'll share three  things with you today  the first is we need to reimagine the  future the alternative to today's  reality  we need to take a step back and ask  ourselves questions like  what are the meaningful habits and the  intentional interactions that we want to  help foster  what are the products that we want our  kids and our grandkids to grow up with  and what does technology that we trust  actually look like  instead we're too focused on the here  and now  the quarterly earnings the annual  revenue  and those short-term views bring us  short-term solutions  so i challenge you to invite your team  to a meeting and brainstorm the idyllic  future  because with a clear vision of the  future that we want to build  we can shift from band-aid solutions to  long-term  transformations the second thing we need  to do  is measure what really matters we have a  natural tendency as technologists to  imagine  all the good that our products will do  for the world but once they launch  and reach scale we don't see the  negatives  we're surprised by them we're so focused  on building the car  that we ignore the traffic  so plan for that wild success treat  those edge cases  as core cases those extreme users as  core users  establish a risk mitigation process that  will help identify and surface  those hard to imagine consequences not  all risks can be avoided  but they can be anticipated  we also need to measure new things most  products have a north star metric  a singular metric that all teams are  striving to optimize  usually this comes in the form of  short-term engagement something like  time spent or number of sessions per day  and as a result the teams are over  optimizing  on moving that needle but when you over  optimize for that short term squeeze  you forget everything that comes at the  cost of that  and so instead of a single north star i  propose a metric constellation a set of  metrics that measures not just  engagement  but also digital well-being value  thresholds  and better usage habits because what  gets measured  gets managed and finally we need to be  champions  of caution silicon valley has a strong  bias for action  a tendency to release something  half-baked and test and iterate and test  and iterate  but while this is great for progress  it's dangerous for society  instead we need to prioritize veracity  over velocity  purpose over speed we need a culture  where it's okay to ask the tough  questions  a culture where we don't hide behind all  the good we're doing  a culture of responsibility because it's  the right thing to do  and it's good business so what does this  actually look like  think back to your team's last product  release  mine was a celebration that included  lots of cake  we would ship something on time and  under budget  but imagine if we set a norm where we  reward teams for shipping something late  because an ethical concern was surfaced  and fixed where we decide not to ship it  at all  because of the major causes it could  have on people  i say that that deserves cake  now it may sound a little bit trivial  but this is exactly the culture that  we'll need  to push the balance of possibility to  define new business models  and discover novel ways to provide value  to users  as technologists we can release a  product and millions of people will be  impacted  just like that technology is not born  bad  it reflects the interests behaviors and  desires  of its creators  as future entrepreneurs and investors  and technologists and change makers  all of you play a role in building the  future of responsible technology  i to be part of the problem to be  passionate about the solution  but whatever drives you i hope you'll  buckle up and join me on the ride  thank you  you

